Throughout this module, I explored how Active Directory (AD) is used to centrally manage users, computers, and security policies in Windows domain environments.
I learned about Domain Controllers and their role in authentication and resource management.

I studied AD components such as users, machine accounts, security groups, and Organizational Units (OUs), 
understanding how they help organize resources and control access. I also learned about delegation,
which allows administrators to assign specific permissions without granting full administrative access.

Another important topic was Group Policy Objects (GPOs), which allow administrators to apply security settings and configurations across users and computers.
I explored how policies can control password requirements, restrict access to system settings, and automatically lock inactive sessions.

I also learned about Windows domain authentication, focusing on Kerberos and NetNTLM, 
as well as how domains can be organized into trees and forests and connected through trust relationships to share resources securely.

This module strengthened my understanding of identity management, authentication, access control, and security administration in Windows environments,
all of which are important skills in Cybersecurity.
